  "summary": "Nearly a year after the Oakland City Council approved a $2.25 million Flock Safety contract with added data-protection safeguards, OPD has not signed the contract, raising questions about whether the safeguards\u2014including limits on out-of-state sharing, quarterly certifications, audits, and $200,000 penalties\u2014are enforceable. The article notes ongoing controversies around Flock, including SFPD's illegal sharing of Oakland data with federal agencies and a recent San Jose officer misuse case.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "Because the contract hasn't been executed, we don't know if any of these safeguards have been implemented, or whether the last nine months will be retroactively included in the contract.",
      "paragraph_idx": 21
    },
    {
      "quote": "The deal the council approved stated that if Flock allows the unauthorized sharing of data, the city can impose up to $200,000 in penalties.",
      "paragraph_idx": 18
    },
    {
      "quote": "Last year, the San Francisco Standard reported that the San Francisco Police Department had illegally shared data acquired from Oakland's Flock system with federal law enforcement agencies.",
      "paragraph_idx": 24
    }
  ],
